---
// VISUAILS — FigAnatomy: what one delivered visual is made of.
//
// This is the /ai-act page's §2 disclosure, drawn. §2 says a visual starts from
// photographs of the client’s real product, that an AI pipeline builds the rest
// of the frame — light, background, model, motion — and that a person finishes
// and checks the result. Four prose paragraphs to establish one fact about
// provenance: which part of the picture was photographed and which part was
// made. That fact is spatial. It belongs in a drawing, and it is the single
// clearest case on the site of task #246's "meer visuele uitleg".
//
// EVERY OTHER FIGURE IN THIS SET DRAWS A PROCESS — a rail, a fan, a track, a
// bar cut into repayments. This one draws the OUTPUT, which is why it does not
// read as any of them and why it could not have been assembled out of the parts
// they already have. A process diagram here would answer "how is it made",
// which /how-it-works already answers twice. The question this page is asked is
// "what am I looking at", and the honest answer is a picture of the thing with
// its parts named.
//
// THE MARKS, AND WHY EACH ONE MEANS WHAT IT MEANS:
//
//   Solid ink        the photographed part — the client’s own product. Solid
//                    because it is the one region that was not invented, and
//                    the eye should find it first without being told to.
//   Hatch            the built part. Hatching is the floor-plan mark for "this
//                    region is filled in", used the same way in FigCapacity. It
//                    is a repeating-linear-gradient because CSS has no hatch
//                    primitive — not a gradient in DESIGN.md's sense, since
//                    nothing is being made to look richer and the pattern is
//                    carrying the meaning.
//   Crop marks       the human pass. Registration marks at two corners are what
//                    a person puts on an image that has been framed and passed
//                    for output; they are a production mark, not an ornament,
//                    which is why they sit on the frame rather than inside it.
//                    They surround BOTH regions on purpose: §2's claim is that
//                    a person checks the result, not that a person checks the
//                    generated half.
//
// WHAT THIS FIGURE MUST NOT DO. The brief's constraint on this page is "never
// turn AI Act page into a fear pitch", and a drawing can breach that more
// quietly than a sentence can. So: no red, no warning mark, no scale or meter
// implying a quantity of artificiality, no before/after pair implying something
// was got away with. Two regions, named, at equal weight. The page's own §6
// refuses to characterise the client’s images in either direction, and a
// diagram that visually editorialised would say what §6 declines to say.
//
// NO PHOTOGRAPH IN HERE, deliberately, and this is the one figure where that
// choice carries an argument rather than a house style. A real product photo
// dropped into the solid region would make the drawing prettier and would also
// make it a specimen — a claim about one actual image — when what it needs to
// be is a claim about the shape of every image. The abstraction is the honesty.
//
// NO MOTION AND NO REVEAL. /ai-act carries no reveal animation because the
// legal pages it sits with carry none, and the page's own markup comment says
// why: a trust document that fades in on scroll is performing. A figure that
// animated in would break that on the one page where tone is the subject.
//
// All copy is passed in from the calling page's bilingual COPY table, so
// check_lang_parity covers EN/NL parity on it for free and no language lives in
// this file. Same contract as every other Fig* component.
//
// The key rows carry an explicit `mark` and the markup tests it three times
// against literal strings rather than building a class name with a template
// literal. That is not clumsiness. class_census, verify3 and orphan_sweep read
// the markup statically; an interpolated `is-${k.mark}` is invisible to all
// three, so the three styles below would be reported as dead and the three
// classes above as unstyled. The idiom here matches FigPipeline's `s.gate &&
// 'is-gate'` for the same reason.
//
// `mark` is typed `string` rather than 'real' | 'built' | 'checked', and that is
// forced by the calling page rather than preferred here. AiActPage's COPY table
// carries no `as const` — its own comment says why: with it, COPY[lang] resolves
// to a union of readonly tuple types and .map() on that union is a TS error, so
// every page component omits it. Without it every string in the table widens to
// `string`, and a literal union in this interface would reject the very table
// this component is fed from. The three values are enforced by the three literal
// comparisons in the markup instead, and an unrecognised mark therefore draws no
// swatch at all — a visible blank beside a term rather than a wrong mark beside
// it. That is the right way for this particular figure to fail. A key row that
// silently drew the solid ink block next to "Built" would be a false provenance
// claim, which is the one error this page cannot afford to make.
interface Key {
  mark: string;
  t: string;
  b: string;
}
interface Props {
  realLabel: string;
  builtLabel: string;
  keys: Key[];
  note?: string;
}
const { realLabel, builtLabel, keys, note } = Astro.props;
---

<figure class="an">
  <div class="an-row">

    {/* Not aria-hidden: the two labels inside it are real text and they carry
        the whole point of the drawing. Only the marks that repeat what the text
        already says are hidden from the accessibility tree. */}
    <div class="an-plate">
      <div class="an-frame">
        <span class="an-built-lab">{builtLabel}</span>
        <div class="an-real">
          <span class="an-real-lab">{realLabel}</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <ul class="an-keys">
      {keys.map((k) => (
        <li class="an-key">
          <span
            class:list={[
              'an-mark',
              k.mark === 'real' && 'is-real',
              k.mark === 'built' && 'is-built',
              k.mark === 'checked' && 'is-checked',
            ]}
            aria-hidden="true"
          ></span>
          <span class="an-key-t">{k.t}</span>
          <span class="an-key-b">{k.b}</span>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>

  </div>
  {note && <figcaption class="an-note">{note}</figcaption>}
</figure>

<style>
  .an { margin: 0; }

  .an-row {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 260px) minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: clamp(1.4rem, 4vw, 3rem);
    align-items: start;
  }

  /* The crop marks live on the plate rather than on the frame so they sit
     OUTSIDE the frame's own border, which is where a registration mark goes.
     Two opposite corners, not four: two is enough to read as registration and
     four starts to look like a decorative bracket, which is the thing this
     page cannot afford. */
  .an-plate { position: relative; padding: 9px; }
  .an-plate::before,
  .an-plate::after {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    width: 16px;
    height: 16px;
    border: 0 solid var(--ink);
  }
  .an-plate::before { top: 0; left: 0; border-top-width: 2px; border-left-width: 2px; }
  .an-plate::after { right: 0; bottom: 0; border-right-width: 2px; border-bottom-width: 2px; }

  /* 4/5 is the ratio the site's own product placeholders use, so the rectangle
     reads as a delivered visual rather than as a generic box. */
  .an-frame {
    position: relative;
    aspect-ratio: 4 / 5;
    border: 1px solid var(--line-strong);
    background: repeating-linear-gradient(
      45deg,
      transparent 0 5px,
      var(--line-strong) 5px 6px
    );
    overflow: hidden;
  }

  /* Knocked out of the hatch with a solid ground behind it — the drafting
     convention for a label over hatching, and the only way small type stays
     readable on a 6px pitch. */
  .an-built-lab {
    position: absolute;
    top: 8px;
    left: 8px;
    max-width: calc(100% - 16px);
    padding: 3px 6px;
    background: var(--bg);
    font-size: .72rem;
    line-height: 1.25;
    color: var(--ink-2);
  }

  /* Standing on the floor of the frame, centred, occupying a minority of it.
     The proportion is the claim: most of what you are looking at was built,
     and the part that was not is the product itself. */
  .an-real {
    position: absolute;
    left: 50%;
    bottom: 0;
    transform: translateX(-50%);
    width: 46%;
    height: 58%;
    background: var(--ink);
    display: flex;
    align-items: flex-end;
    justify-content: center;
    padding: 8px;
  }
  .an-real-lab {
    font-size: .72rem;
    line-height: 1.25;
    text-align: center;
    color: var(--bg);
  }

  .an-keys {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: clamp(1rem, 2.4vw, 1.5rem);
  }

  /* Two columns: the mark keeps its own gutter so the three terms align on one
     edge regardless of how wide each mark is drawn. */
  .an-key {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 22px minmax(0, 1fr);
    column-gap: .9rem;
    align-items: start;
  }

  .an-mark { width: 22px; height: 22px; grid-row: span 2; }
  .is-real { background: var(--ink); }
  .is-built {
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    background: repeating-linear-gradient(
      45deg,
      transparent 0 4px,
      var(--line-strong) 4px 5px
    );
  }

  /* The same two-corner registration mark as the plate, at swatch size, so the
     key is drawn with the mark it is explaining rather than with a stand-in. */
  .is-checked { position: relative; }
  .is-checked::before,
  .is-checked::after {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    width: 9px;
    height: 9px;
    border: 0 solid var(--ink);
  }
  .is-checked::before { top: 0; left: 0; border-top-width: 2px; border-left-width: 2px; }
  .is-checked::after { right: 0; bottom: 0; border-right-width: 2px; border-bottom-width: 2px; }

  .an-key-t { font-size: .95rem; font-weight: 600; color: var(--ink); }
  .an-key-b {
    grid-column: 2;
    margin-top: .3rem;
    font-size: .85rem;
    line-height: 1.55;
    color: var(--ink-2);
    max-width: 46ch;
  }

  .an-note {
    margin-top: 1.4rem;
    padding-top: .8rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--line);
    font-size: .82rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--ink-3);
    max-width: 62ch;
  }

  /* Below the fold-point the frame goes full width and the key follows it. The
     frame keeps its ratio, so it is capped rather than left to grow to the full
     column — a page-tall rectangle of hatching would be the drawing shouting,
     and this page in particular must not. */
  @media (max-width: 720px) {
    .an-row { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
    .an-plate { max-width: 300px; }
  }
</style>
